arm64: dts: qcom: sm7150: parent the GCC power domains to CX

The USB controller (usb@a6f8800) and the UFS host vote required-opps NOM on
their GCC GDSCs (USB30_PRIM_GDSC, UFS_PHY_GDSC), but the GCC node carries no
power-domains property, so those GDSCs are not subdomains of the rpmhpd CX
domain and the votes never reach CX. Measured on the POCO X3 NFC (surya),
2026-09-08: pm_genpd_summary shows usb30_prim_gdsc at state 256 as an orphan
while CX only lists the camcc/dispcc/videocc GDSCs as children.

In practice CX was held at NOM only by the Bluetooth UART running at
3.2 Mbaud (qup OPP 128 MHz -> NOM). Anything that lowers that vote (bluetoothd
powering the adapter off, rfkill) drops CX to the display's SVS_L1 while the
USB controller keeps running its 200 MHz master clock, and the SoC freezes
without a trace until the watchdog resets it. Reproduced with the console UART
as a substitute NOM vote: with CX pinned, 6 Bluetooth power cycles survive;
dropping the pin with Bluetooth off kills the SoC immediately.

Do what sc7180 does and put GCC under CX so the GDSC performance states
propagate (gdsc_register() adds every GDSC as a subdomain of the controller's
own power domain).

--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm7150.dtsi
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm7150.dtsi
@@ -926,6 +926,8 @@
 				 <&rpmhcc RPMH_CXO_CLK_A>,
 				 <&sleep_clk>;
 
+			power-domains = <&rpmhpd RPMHPD_CX>;
+
 			#clock-cells = <1>;
 			#reset-cells = <1>;
 			#power-domain-cells = <1>;
